Let $E$ denote the given expression. Use $\sin x\cos x=\frac{1}{2}\sin(2x)$ first at $x=7.5^\circ$ and then at $x=15^\circ$. Each application introduces a factor of $\frac{1}{2}$.
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=2\,\sin\left(15^\circ\right)\cos\left(15^\circ\right)\\&=\sin\left(30^\circ\right)
\end{aligned}
$$
The terminal sine is $\sin\left(30^\circ\right)=\frac{1}{2}$.
Therefore,
$$
\begin{aligned}
E&=\left(\frac{1}{2}\right)\\&=\frac{1}{2}.
\end{aligned}
$$

